  <section>
    <h2>The problem</h2>
    <p class="c-lead">Smart thermostats save energy. Building, running and connecting them costs energy too.</p>
    <p>
      The IoT Sustainability Lab at HTWG Konstanz wanted to put numbers on both sides over the whole life of a device,
      with a life cycle assessment. The software it had in mind simulated the setup: which devices, connected by WLAN or
      by Bluetooth. That answers how a system is wired, not what it costs once people live with it.
    </p>
    <p>
      The question a tool is built around decides what it can ever show. That is as true for this lab as it is for
      Levelwork.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>What I did</h2>
    <p class="c-lead">A sprint with the lab to find the real question, then three rounds of wireframes.</p>
    <p>
      I planned and ran a design sprint with the lab team. We wrote “how might we” questions, mapped who would use the
      tool, from device makers and facility managers to climate officers and people at home, laid out what each of them
      brings in, what has to be calculated and what they need out, and drew the path from a question to a printed
      result. From there I drew the wireframes, in March, May and June.
    </p>
    <div class="c-pair">
      <figure>
        <Shot
          src={hmw}
          source="Design sprint, March 2024"
          label="How might we"
          alt="Thirteen pink sticky notes on a white wall, how-might-we questions such as how might we exclude greenwashing and how might we include the sustainability of the device itself"
          widths={[800, 1600]}
          sizes={half}
        />
        <figcaption>Thirteen questions, among them: exclude greenwashing, include the device itself.</figcaption>
      </figure>
      <figure>
        <Shot
          src={storyboard}
