        <figcaption>Expert and non-expert view, devices, users, cloud.</figcaption>
      </figure>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>Decisions</h2>
    <p class="c-lead">From the wiring to the use: rooms, people and how often they reach for the data.</p>
    <p>
      <strong>Model the use, not the network.</strong> The first wireframe put the network in the middle: cloud, router,
      gateway, lamps and heaters, each link marked WLAN or Bluetooth. The later ones model three things that act on each
      other: the rooms and when they are used, the people, whether they are active or passive, how long they are at home
      and which device they connect with, and the IoT services. A switch sets how much the system talks to the cloud
      server.
    </p>
    <figure class="c-wide">
      <ScrubFigure kind="iot-model" ratio="1200 / 560" narrow="600 / 920" />
      <figcaption>The March network sorts itself into rooms, people and IoT services; the switch sets how often the phones' requests travel through the network to the cloud.</figcaption>
    </figure>
    <p>
      <strong>Results in the language of a life cycle assessment.</strong> The results page splits the footprint into
      production, operation and end of life, shows carbon and energy demand for high, normal and low use, and lists the
      impact categories of a life cycle assessment instead of a single green score.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>Start from a template.</strong> Simple, average or complex household, or an upload, so that nobody starts
      from an empty canvas.
    </p>
    <div class="c-pair">
      <figure>
        <Shot
          src={before}
          source="Wireframe, March 2024"
          label="Before"
          alt="Early wireframe: a network from the cloud through a router and gateway to lamps and heaters, each link marked with a Bluetooth or WLAN symbol"
          widths={[800, 1600]}
          sizes={half}
        />
        <figcaption>March: the wiring in the middle, Bluetooth or WLAN on every link.</figcaption>
      </figure>
      <figure>
        <Shot
          src={after}
          source="Wireframe, June 2024"
          label="After"
          alt="Later wireframe: environment with rooms and their hours of use, users who are active or passive with their connection devices, IoT services, and a switch for cloud server interaction from high to low and green energy"
          widths={[800, 1600]}
          sizes={half}
        />
        <figcaption>June: rooms, people and devices acting on each other, and how often the cloud is asked.</figcaption>
      </figure>
    </div>
    <figure class="c-wide">
      <Shot source="Model view, redrawn 2026" label="From the June 2024 wireframe">
        <IotModelScreen />
      </Shot>
      <figcaption>The model view as a finished screen, redrawn from the June wireframe.</figcaption>
    </figure>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>Result</h2>
    <p class="c-lead">The biggest lever was not the device. It was how often people look at their data.</p>
    <p>
      Once use was in the model, the calculations showed the network and the cloud standing out: every time someone checks the heating on their
      phone, data travels through the network, and access and transfer take the most energy. The lab then turned its
      work towards the ecological footprint of the whole system.
    </p>
    <p>
      The wireframes were the brief for the lab's programmers, who were to build the tool. I was not part of that. In
      September 2026 the lab presented a software that simulates the ecological performance of IoT systems in building
      automation, IoT ecoSIM, at its conference in Vaduz. How much of the 2024 concept is in it, I can't tell from the
      outside.
    </p>
    <figure class="c-wide">
      <Shot source="Results view, redrawn 2026" label="From the June 2024 wireframe">
        <IotResultsScreen />
      </Shot>
      <figcaption>The results as a finished screen: by life phase and impact category, the cloud with the largest share.</figcaption>
    </figure>
    <dl class="c-numbers">
      <div><dt>13</dt><dd>how-might-we questions from the sprint</dd></div>
      <div><dt>6</dt><dd>roles the tool serves, from device makers to climate officers</dd></div>
      <div><dt>3</dt><dd>rounds of wireframes in fourteen weeks</dd></div>
    </dl>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>Timeline</h2>
    <ol class="c-timeline">
